  AI coding tools are confusing, as there is constant change and a myriad of features that you may not use. So, I decided to [compare](https://github.com/murphye/ai-coding-tools-comparison-guide) [Cursor](https://www.cursor.com/), [Claude Code](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code), [Copilot](https://github.com/features/copilot), [Windsurf](https://windsurf.com/), [Antigravity](https://idx.google.com/antigravity), [Kiro](https://kiro.dev/), [Codex CLI](https://github.com/openai/codex), and [Gemini CLI](https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli) to figure out how they stack up so you can decide which is the best fit for you. For me, I use multiple tools, so knowing their pricing, strengths, and gaps is very important to optimize my workflows.
  
  The full, detailed comparison is located on [GitHub](https://github.com/murphye/ai-coding-tools-comparison-guide).
  
  Press enter or click to view image in full size
  
  ![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*SlDdnxVWpIPIOig9r0watA.png)
  
  ## What You’ll Actually Pay
  
  Pricing was the biggest source of confusion from my research. The sticker price rarely tells the full story.
  
  **Antigravity** (Google) launched free with generous limits, but Pro users have seen weekly token budgets drop by roughly significantly since launch. The credit costs for coding tasks still aren’t documented, and there’s a [**huge** gap](https://gemini.google/subscriptions/) between the $20 Pro plan and $250 Ultra with nothing in between.
  
  **Claude Code** uses flat-rate pricing on its Max plans. One developer [tracked](https://www.ksred.com/claude-code-pricing-guide-which-plan-actually-saves-you-money/) 10 billion tokens over 8 months at $100/month on Max. The same usage on per-token API rates would have cost around $15,000. Predictable billing with no surprises.
  
  **Cursor** Pro is $20/month with extended agent request limits, but how far that goes depends heavily on which model you use. Higher tiers include Pro+ ($60/month, 3x usage) and Ultra ($200/month, 20x usage). Pay-as-you-go overages can add up quickly, so keep an eye on your usage dashboard.
  
  **Copilot** is the cheapest entry at $10/month for Pro. The free tier is genuinely useful too: 2,000 completions plus 50 premium requests per month. But be aware that advanced models like Opus eat 3x premium requests per use.
  
  **Windsurf** Pro at $15/month gives you about 1,000 prompts per month, and tab completions are truly unlimited at no credit cost. Solid value if the basic model quality meets your needs.
  
  **Codex CLI** on the $20 Plus plan can be exhausted in as few as two 10-minute sessions. The 1M context window requires the $200 Pro plan. Codex GPT-5.4 has had positive reviews from developers, compared to previous versions what were generally far behind Claude Code.
  
  **Gemini CLI** advertises 1,000 free requests per day, but those mostly hit Flash (the lighter model). Some users report rate-limiting on Gemini Pro after just 4 to 15 large prompts. The only way to guarantee Pro model access is bringing your own API key.
  
  **Kiro** uses a variable credit system where different prompts cost different amounts depending on the model. AWS has acknowledged bugs that drained developer limits unexpectedly.
  
  ## Features That Actually Differentiate
  
  A lot of features are table stakes now. Multi-agent support, plan mode, custom rules, and MCP server integration are everywhere. Here’s what actually sets tools apart.
  
