  Remember me for faster sign in
  
  **Context window usage matters more than size.** Claude Code, Gemini CLI, and Codex CLI all offer 1M token windows. But Windsurf’s effective context is only about 50–70K tokens despite advertising larger windows, and Cursor defaults to 200K. How intelligently a tool retrieves and uses context matters far more than the raw number.
  
  **Sandboxing varies wildly.** Codex CLI has Linux kernel-level sandboxing on by default. Claude Code uses namespace-based isolation with network blocking. Cursor added OS-level sandboxing in early 2026, reducing permission prompts by about 40%. Windsurf has no documented kernel sandbox and relies on user approval prompts. If you’re giving an AI agent the ability to run code and modify files, this matters.
  
  **Flexible Developer experience.** Pick between a purely CLI-driven approach (i.e. Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex CLI), a fully agentic IDE (i.e. Cursor, Antigravity), or something in between (i.e. Claude Code plugin for VS Code).
  
  **Spec-driven development.** Kiro generates structured requirements, design docs, and task breakdowns before writing any code. It’s the only tool that creates an auditable trail from requirement to implementation. This is a real differentiator for teams in regulated industries or anyone who needs traceability.
  
  ## Picking the Right Tool for Your Workflow
  
  After going through all of this, the “best” tool depends entirely on how you work.
  
  **Solo dev, predictable costs:** Claude Code Max. Flat rate, strongest reasoning quality (Opus 4.6 with 1M context), no billing surprises.
  
  **Cheapest entry into agentic coding:** Copilot Pro at $10/month. Nothing else comes close on price.
  
  **Full IDE experience with the biggest ecosystem:** Cursor. 1 million daily active users, 50K+ businesses, most polished editor integration.
  
  **Best free tier for CLI work:** Gemini CLI (1,000 req/day, mostly Flash) combined with Copilot Free (2,000 completions + 50 premium requests).
  
  **Parallel agents and heavy agentic workflows:** Antigravity (up to parallel agents), Cursor (8 parallel agents), or Kiro (up to 10 subagents), depending on your budget and ecosystem preference.
  
  **Team that needs audit trails and compliance:** Kiro. The spec-driven workflow with structured requirements is unique and valuable for regulated environments.
  
  ## The Practical Takeaway
  
  Three things stood out from this whole project.
  
  First, watch your billing. Multiple tools have changed pricing models or reduced quotas within their first year. Flat-rate plans remove that risk entirely.
  
  Second, try before you commit. Almost every tool here has a free tier or trial. Your actual workflow will tell you more than any feature matrix. The tool that handles your specific codebase and patterns well is the right one, regardless of benchmarks.
  
  Third, portability is real. Custom rules and skills files use similar formats across tools. One developer reported moving skills from Claude Code into Antigravity without modification. You’re less locked in than you might think.
  
  The [full comparison guide](https://github.com/murphye/ai-coding-tools-comparison-guide) has detailed breakdowns for each tool, including pricing scenarios, feature tables, and known limitations. Hopefully this gives you a practical starting point for figuring out what fits your work.
  
